      "reason": "The question explicitly defines the Niño 3.4 index as monthly tos/SST anomalies relative to each source’s own 1950–2014 monthly climatology, so the anomaly baseline and index construction directly determine the requested amplitudes and exceedance counts.",
      "status": "condensed",
      "notes": [
        "Compute each Niño 3.4 series as the monthly SST anomaly over 5S–5N, 170W–120W relative to that source’s **own** 1950–2014 monthly climatology; state the climatology and baseline, since anomalies on different baselines differ by a monthly offset and CPC products on other baselines are not interchangeable.",
        "Niño 3.4 is a monthly index matched to its own calendar month climatology; for the month-by-month amplitudes, compare January with January, …, December with December, and assess exceedance counts on the anomalies, not on raw SST."
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "cmip6-tos-bulk-top-cell",
      "severity": "should",
      "path": "concepts/cmip6-tos-bulk-top-cell.md",
      "sha256": "4acdeba0f49784d4435dbc4eed34ca9777254313daa951641759fa02df24cf64",
      "reason": "The CMIP6 variable being assessed is tos, and this fragment matters because tos is the model top-cell bulk ocean temperature rather than a skin SST, which is a needed caveat when comparing model “SST” amplitude against ERSSTv5.",
      "status": "condensed",
      "notes": [
        "`tos` is the model surface temperature to use for the CMIP6 historical runs; it is the bulk temperature of the top model cell, not skin SST, so compare like-for-like with ERSSTv5 as an SST product and do not substitute another depth definition here.",
        "Sea-ice-covered cells in `tos` are valid under-ice water temperatures, not missing; the Niño 3.4 regional mean should therefore use the model field’s actual valid-data mask, not an added sea-ice mask."
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "omip-weighted-means-and-integrals",
      "severity": "should",
      "path": "methods/omip-weighted-means-and-integrals.md",
      "sha256": "89de52676ae54db8f5897bc77a08c78de3f776696db0020935a38b790c302cbe",
      "reason": "Niño 3.4 is an area mean over 5°S–5°N, 170°W–120°W, and with each source kept on its native grid and published cell areas, the regional mean must be area-weighted rather than an unweighted average of grid cells.",
      "status": "condensed",
      "notes": [
        "Form each monthly Niño 3.4 SST anomaly from an **area-weighted** mean over 5S–5N, 170W–120W on that source’s own grid: `Σ tos · areacello / Σ areacello` over valid ocean cells; plain `mean()` over `(y, x)` is wrong.",
        "Mask weights to the same valid cells as `tos` for every monthly regional mean; the denominator must exclude land/missing cells."
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "monthly-means-are-time-averages",
      "severity": "must",
      "path": "pitfalls/monthly-means-are-time-averages.md",
      "sha256": "df6b5c23460f88f1522e5c6a665e1efbb8322c3ae876c885530f3019aa284ee0",
      "reason": "The calculation uses monthly CMIP6 and ERSST time series, so it is relevant that the monthly values are already time averages, which constrains how the index and its standard deviation are interpreted.",
      "status": "condensed",
      "notes": [
        "The input monthly `tos` fields are already monthly time means; no extra day-weighting is needed for the 1950–2014 monthly climatology used for anomalies, because that climatology is the plain mean of each calendar month across years.",
        "The requested standard deviations are over the 780 monthly Niño 3.4 anomalies (and separately within each calendar month across years), i.e. variability of monthly means, not sub-monthly variability."
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "cmip6-grid-labels",
